# 01 — Introduction to Playwright

## What it is
Playwright is a free, open-source tool from Microsoft for automating web browsers. I use it to write end-to-end tests that behave like a real user: open a page, click, type, and check the result.

## Why teams choose it
- Works with Chromium (Chrome/Edge), Firefox and WebKit (Safari engine) using the same code
- Waits for elements automatically, so tests fail less often for random timing reasons
- Comes with a test runner, assertions, reports, screenshots and a trace viewer
- Can also test APIs and mock network calls
- Runs tests in parallel and works well in CI pipelines
- Languages: JavaScript/TypeScript, Python, Java, .NET

## How it is built (simple view)
Test script → Playwright → Browser

Hierarchy to remember:
Browser → Browser Context (isolated session) → Page (tab) → Locator → Element

Every test gets a fresh context, so tests do not affect each other.

## Playwright vs Selenium vs Cypress
| | Playwright | Selenium | Cypress |
|---|---|---|---|
| Waiting | Automatic | Manual waits needed | Automatic |
| Test runner | Built in | Needs TestNG/JUnit/pytest | Built in |
| Multiple tabs | Yes | Yes | Limited |
| Languages | JS/TS, Python, Java, .NET | Many | JS/TS only |
| Parallel runs | Built in, free | Needs Grid | Paid or plugins |

## Setup
Requires Node.js (LTS).

    npm init playwright@latest

Choose TypeScript, the `tests` folder, and install the browsers when asked.

## My first test
```typescript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('homepage has the right title', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://playwright.dev/');
  await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Playwright/);
});
```

Run it:

    npx playwright test
    npx playwright show-report

## Things I learned
- Always use `await`. Forgetting it is the most common beginner mistake.
- `{ page }` is a fixture. Playwright gives each test its own clean page.
- Use `@playwright/test` for testing. The plain `playwright` package is only the automation library.

